## 00:00 Before an LLM Can Learn

You have probably seen AI improve an email, rewrite a paragraph, summarize a document, or suggest a piece of code.

You type some text, and the AI gives you new text.

In this course, we are going to build and train that AI from scratch, step by step. This type of is called Large Language Model or LLM in short

LLM It's a AI system trained on huge amounts of text (books, websites, articles) to learn patterns in how language works — which words tend to follow other words, how ideas connect, how questions get answered, and so on

Since LLMs learns from we need to....

Since LLMs learn from text, we first need to collect the text they will learn from.

One sentence or one document is not enough. We need many examples of writing from sources such as books, articles, websites, and other documents.

When we collect and organise these examples for training, we call the collection a **training dataset**.

But we cannot simply collect the text and immediately give it to the LLM.

Real-world text can be messy.

It may contain extra spaces, repeated blank lines, tabs, unusual symbols, hidden characters, or different characters that look almost the same.

For example, look at this text:

[On screen: Show the sample as it normally appears.]

```text
  Lesson ①: Ａ cat ﬀ

  second line
```

At first, it looks like two simple lines of text.

But the computer may receive more than we can easily see.

There may be spaces at the beginning and end.

The gap before `second line` may be a tab instead of ordinary spaces.

The empty line may be created by hidden line-boundary characters.

Some visible characters may also be different from the ordinary characters we expect.

For example:

```text
①    1
Ａ    A
ﬀ    ff
```

These forms may look similar to us, but the computer may store them as different characters.

Before we train the LLM, we therefore need to answer two important questions:

> **What exactly is contained in the source text?**

And:

> **Which differences should we keep, and which should we change?**

This lesson is about answering those questions.

We will inspect the source text, identify the characters it contains, and prepare it consistently for the next stages of training.

The full course will follow a path like this:

```text
collect text
→ organise the training dataset
→ inspect and prepare the text
→ split the text into smaller pieces
→ represent those pieces with numbers
→ create training examples
→ build the LLM
→ train the LLM
→ test the LLM
```

Each step prepares the material for the next step.

Today, we are focusing on one early part of that process:

```text
source text
→ inspect it
→ identify important differences
→ choose preparation rules
→ apply the rules consistently
→ prepared text
```

Why is this important?

An LLM is a mathematical system. Its calculations work with numbers.

Later, we will split the text into pieces called **tokens** and represent those tokens using numbers.

But before we do that, we need to understand the text we are starting with.

Otherwise, we may carry hidden problems and inconsistencies into the later stages of training.

At the same time, we should not assume that every difference is a mistake.

A special symbol, a blank line, or unusual spacing may be important in one dataset and unnecessary in another.

So we do not change the text blindly.

We inspect it first.

Then we decide what to preserve and what to change based on the purpose of our dataset.

That gives us the main question for this lesson:

> **How can we find out exactly what a piece of text contains and prepare it consistently before using it in LLM training?**

