## 02:30 Reveal What Is Really Stored

Let us begin with the exact text stored by the program.

We will keep the original text unchanged while we inspect it.

We call this unchanged starting text the **source text**, or simply the **source**.

```python
source = "  Lesson ①: Ａ cat ﬀ  \r\n\r\n\tsecond line  "
```

Python calls a stored piece of text like this a **string**.

The quotation marks tell Python where the string begins and ends.

The quotation marks themselves are not part of the stored text.

Inside the string, we can see some special forms:

```text
\t
\r\n
```

The form `\t` represents a tab.

The form `\r\n` represents a line boundary.

Two `\r\n` line boundaries in a row create an empty line.

The equals sign stores the value on the right under the name `source`.

```python
source = "  Lesson ①: Ａ cat ﬀ  \r\n\r\n\tsecond line  "
```

Normally, when we print text, some of these details may be difficult to see.

For example, spaces at the beginning or end can be easy to miss.

A tab may look like several ordinary spaces.

Different kinds of line boundaries may all appear as a new line on the screen.

So before running the program, think about this question:

> What might normal printing hide about the beginning, the end, and the space between the lines?

Python gives us a useful tool called `repr`.

`repr` displays the string in a way that makes hidden details easier to see.

```python
print(repr(source))
```

The result is:

```text
'  Lesson ①: Ａ cat ﬀ  \r\n\r\n\tsecond line  '
```

Now we can see the structure more clearly.

The source contains:

```text
two spaces before Lesson
two spaces after ﬀ
two line boundaries
a tab before second
two spaces after line
```

The outer quotation marks were added by `repr` to show us where the string starts and ends.

Those quotation marks are not part of the stored value.

Spaces, tabs, and line boundaries all create gaps in text.

Together, we call them **whitespace**.

Using tools such as `repr` to reveal what the source contains is called **inspection**.

Inspection does not change the source.

It only helps us see what is already stored.

We have now revealed some hidden whitespace.

But there is another issue.

Some visible characters may look similar even though the computer stores them as different characters.

