## 15:15 What We Have Learned

We can now answer the main question from the beginning of the lesson:

> **How can we find out exactly what a piece of text contains and prepare it consistently before using it in LLM training?**

We begin with the unchanged source text.

We inspect it to reveal hidden whitespace and stored character identities.

We then decide which differences matter for the purpose of our dataset.

After choosing the rules, we apply them consistently and test the result using more than one example.

The complete process is:

```text
source text
→ inspect what is stored
→ identify important differences
→ choose rules for the purpose
→ apply the rules
→ test the result
→ prepared text
```

The most important distinction is this:

> **Identification tells us what is present. Preparation decides what to do with it.**

A Unicode code point can prove that two characters are different.

But it cannot tell us whether we should make them the same.

That decision depends on the purpose of our dataset.

Do not assume that every difference should be removed.

Some differences may carry useful information.

Others may simply be accidental inconsistencies.

We inspect the source first so that our decisions are deliberate.

At this stage, we have not trained the LLM.

We have also not created tokens, token IDs, or embeddings.

We have only prepared a more consistent piece of text that the later stages can work with.

In the next lesson, we will begin moving from complete text towards the smaller pieces that an LLM can process.